Track 1: Who Marked Five O'Clock in Charcoal?
- Story & Context: When Doña Celia accidentally dropped her battery-powered SP-404 into a warm vat of cinnamon atole at the Cuemanco supply docks, the machine's internal clock circuit warped into a drunken, half-speed wobble that she refused to recalibrate. Instead, she looped a decayed Nortec tuba strike over the glitching metronome, coaxing a mournful, breath-heavy clarinet solo from her grandnephew while rival flower vendors hurled rotten cabbage heads at her barge for disturbing their morning inventory. The floating argument dissolved into silence when the low brass frequency caused water droplets to dance across a hundred metal hulls simultaneously, codifying the very first rhythm of the scene. Her filtered, pitch-shifted hook crackles across the mist: "The calendar rots while we wait for the sun to bargain."
